# .worktreeinclude - gitignored files copied into every new worktree Claude Code creates.
#
# WHY (2026-09-06, backlog E7). A fresh worktree is BLIND, and blind here does not mean loud. The
# gitignored data never arrives, the checks that need it cannot express their finding, and several of
# them return a clean-looking result anyway. Two standing memories exist about this
# ([[run-gates-blind-in-worktrees]], [[worktrees-lack-the-boards-the-engines-price-on]]) and the fix
# was a manual copy nobody remembered to do.
#
# EVERY LINE HERE WAS MEASURED, NOT GUESSED. A worktree at 2fdb99cf ran ops\run-gates.ps1 and failed
# SIX self-tests that pass in the main checkout. Each entry below names the ones it fixed:
#
#   183 passed, 6 failed   bare worktree
#   +db\built              feed-covers-published, wave-preaudit      -> 185 passed
#   +catalog-digest.json   find-similar, make-saturation             -> 187 passed
#
# Syntax is gitignore-style patterns, matched against `git ls-files --others --ignored
# --exclude-standard --directory`. So ONLY IGNORED FILES ARE ELIGIBLE - a tracked file is already in
# the worktree and cannot be listed here. That matters for the two failures this file does NOT fix;
# see the note at the bottom.

# ---- the gate's missing inputs (measured above) -------------------------------------------------
# 115 KB. find-similar and make-saturation both carry a MUST FIRE asserting the digest exists where the
# sourcer prompts will point. Without it the dedup shortlist has nothing to score against.
meal-prep/pipeline/catalog-digest.json

# meal-prep/db/built/ IS DELIBERATELY NOT LISTED HERE, and this is the sharpest limit of the mechanism.
# The candidate list is built with `--directory`, which COLLAPSES a fully-ignored directory to a single
# entry - `meal-prep/db/built/` arrives as one line, not as its 1,168 files. The copier then calls
# copyFile on it, which cannot take a directory, so it logs "Failed to copy ... to worktree" and moves
# on. Measured, not assumed: grocery/out/ enumerates file by file (something tracked lives under it) and
# meal-prep/db/built/ collapses. A pattern here would look right and copy nothing, which is the worst
# available outcome. ops\seed-worktree.ps1 carries that directory instead.

# ---- the boards the pricing engines read --------------------------------------------------------
# NOT a run-gates failure, and on the list anyway, because this is the failure that is silent. run-gates
# deliberately runs only what is hermetic, so it never touches a board and never notices one missing.
# cost-recipes does: with no board it prices nothing and EXITS 0, which reads as success
# ([[worktrees-lack-the-boards-the-engines-price-on]]). A worktree that cannot fail loudly is worse than
# one that fails.
#
# THE COST IS VISIBLE ON PURPOSE: 39 files, 127 MB, because gitignore patterns cannot express "the newest
# one" and the engines want the newest. If per-worktree disk ever becomes the binding constraint, the
# narrowing is a dated pattern (comparison-2026-09-*.json) and the price of that is remembering to move
# it - which is the kind of maintenance this estate has already proven it does not do.
grocery/out/comparison-*.json

# ---- WHAT THIS FILE CANNOT FIX ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Two of the six worktree failures are not missing data and no entry here will help, because both are
# TRACKED files and only ignored files are eligible:
#
#   meal-prep\engine\golden-test.ps1   FAIL "the FROZEN inputs changed - the fixture, not the engine,
#                                      moved". Measured: db\label-folds.json is 383 bytes in the main
#                                      checkout and 390 in the worktree, same 8 lines. That is 7 CR
#                                      bytes, not an edit.
#   grocery\audit-ghost-drift.ps1      FAIL on budget-tracker-tool.html, 28,965 bytes committed vs
#                                      29,358 in the working tree.
#
# Both are the standing condition in [[fresh-checkout-is-crlf-main-is-lf]]: .gitattributes carries
# `* text=auto` and core.autocrlf is true, so a fresh checkout normalises to CRLF while main's working
# tree still sits at LF from when those files were written. The two go red over BYTES, not drift, and
# `git diff` shows nothing ([[crlf-flip-is-invisible-in-git-diff]]). Fixing it is a .gitattributes
# question and a separate piece of work - recorded here so the next person to read a red worktree gate
# does not spend the afternoon hunting for a data file that was never the problem.
